  "caption_md": "Simplified biosynthetic map. Fixed carbon feeds the [cytosolic](#term:cytosol) [mevalonate](#term:mva-pathway) (MVA) and [plastidic](#term:plastid) [MEP](#term:mep-pathway) pathways, which make the [C5 units](#term:ipp-dmapp) of all [terpenoids](#term:terpenoid), and the [shikimate pathway](#term:shikimate-pathway), which makes phenylalanine and tyrosine for the [phenylpropanoid](#term:phenylpropanoid) and [flavonoid](#term:flavonoid) branches. End products are the curated compounds of this site; dashed edges compress several steps, and two edges mark [artefacts](#term:artefact) formed after the plant (carnosol on drying, chamazulene on [steam distillation](#term:steam-distillation)).",
  "how_to_read_md": "Start at the top with the carbon [Rubisco](#term:rubisco) fixes and follow it down one of three trunks. On the terpenoid trunk, the length of the prenyl chain decides the class: ten carbons for the [monoterpenes](#term:monoterpene) of mint, sage, thyme and basil, fifteen for the [sesquiterpenes](#term:sesquiterpene) of chamomile and lemon balm, twenty for rosemary's carnosic acid and thirty for ursolic acid. On the phenolic trunk, phenylalanine becomes cinnamic acid and then p-coumaric acid, which splits three ways: towards rosmarinic acid, towards basil's eugenol and [estragole](#term:estragole), and, with three malonyl-CoA units, into the flavonoids. Each coloured end node opens its compound page. Every edge carries the reference it was drawn from; hover it to see which.",
  "provenance": "synthesised: conceptual diagram"
 },
